described  that describes our observations and on  the right we have the  likelihood which is the probability of  our y values giving the x values and our  model  now we know that this likelihood  this is just going to be our normal  distribution our gaussian distribution  because that's the assumption behind  behind gaussian process  so that's very simple to estimate now  the prior is just some prior knowledge  that we have on our model  or in this case on the parameters of our  model  let's say we know that the models cannot  have some negative values  so that's some prior knowledge that we  incorporate in here  and evidence is just the probability of  what the y value given  x value and in general we focus on the  likelihood and  prior because we want to maximize the  posterior  so the probability of our model being  the true model that describes the data  and for that we need to maximize the  likelihood  and the posterior is going to make the  predictions afterwards  so if we want to predict a new data  point that we haven't seen before  so let's say interpolate or extrapolate  that's what what  the posterior is going to be doing  now caution process model consists of a  main function  and a covariance matrix also called  kerner  in main function it can be anything can  be constant  can be a straight line whatever you want  and  the covariance matrix well the kernel  can have  different forms this is just one example  this is the  square exponential kernel which looks  like this  and it has two main parameters so  it has the variance and the length scale  what this covariance matrix or kernel is  telling us is just the correlation  between  a set of or two data points so if you  have two data points very close together  then the correlation is going to be  quite high  but if the distance between the relative  distance between two data points  is very large then the correlation  between poles is going to be very low  however the exact shape of this function  of t